{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n \\\"markup\\\\.render|Text\\\\.from_markup|from_markup|\\\\.plain|markup\\\" docs/source rich/markup.py rich/text.py | head -80\"","aggregated_output":"docs/source/introduction.rst:49:Strings may contain :ref:`console_markup` which can be used to insert color and styles in to the output.\ndocs/source/introduction.rst:51:The following demonstrates both console markup and pretty formatting of Python objects::\ndocs/source/reference.rst:20:   reference/markup.rst\nrich/markup.py:21:    \"\"\"A tag in console markup.\"\"\"\nrich/markup.py:34:    def markup(self) -> str:\nrich/markup.py:49:    markup: str,\nrich/markup.py:52:    \"\"\"Escapes text so that it won't be interpreted as markup.\nrich/markup.py:55:        markup (str): Content to be inserted in to markup.\nrich/markup.py:66:    markup = _escape(escape_backslashes, markup)\nrich/markup.py:67:    if markup.endswith(\"\\\\\") and not markup.endswith(\"\\\\\\\\\"):\nrich/markup.py:68:        return markup + \"\\\\\"\nrich/markup.py:70:    return markup\nrich/markup.py:73:def _parse(markup: str) -> Iterable[Tuple[int, Optional[str], Optional[Tag]]]:\nrich/markup.py:74:    \"\"\"Parse markup in to an iterable of tuples of (position, text, tag).\nrich/markup.py:77:        markup (str): A string containing console markup\nrich/markup.py:83:    for match in RE_TAGS.finditer(markup):\nrich/markup.py:87:            yield start, markup[position:start], None\nrich/markup.py:102:    if position < len(markup):\nrich/markup.py:103:        yield position, markup[position:], None\nrich/markup.py:107:    markup: str,\nrich/markup.py:112:    \"\"\"Render console markup in to a Text instance.\nrich/markup.py:115:        markup (str): A string containing console markup.\nrich/markup.py:122:        MarkupError: If there is a syntax error in the markup.\nrich/markup.py:128:    if \"[\" not in markup:\nrich/markup.py:130:            emoji_replace(markup, default_variant=emoji_variant) if emoji else markup,\nrich/markup.py:153:    for position, plain_text, tag in _parse(markup):\nrich/markup.py:168:                            f\"closing tag '{tag.markup}' at position {position} doesn't match any open tag\"\nrich/markup.py:248:    for markup in MARKUP:\nrich/markup.py:249:        grid.add_row(Text(markup), markup)\ndocs/source/console.rst:48:To write rich content to the terminal use the :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` method. Rich will convert any object to a string via its (``__str__``) method and perform some simple syntax highlighting. It will also do pretty printing of any containers, such as dicts and lists. If you print a string it will render :ref:`console_markup`. Here are some examples::\ndocs/source/console.rst:97:In additional to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` and :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`, Rich has an :meth:`~rich.console.Console.out` method which provides a lower-level way of writing to the terminal. The out() method converts all the positional arguments to strings and won't pretty print, word wrap, or apply markup to the output, but can apply a basic style and will optionally do highlighting.\ndocs/source/console.rst:266:You can open the SVG in a web browser. You can also insert it in to a webpage with an ``<img>`` tag or by copying the markup in to your HTML.\ndocs/source/console.rst:387:                Text.from_markup(f\"[blink]Don't Panic![/blink]\\n{count}\", justify=\"center\"),\ndocs/source/markup.rst:1:.. _console_markup:\ndocs/source/markup.rst:6:Rich supports a simple markup which you can use to insert color and styles virtually everywhere Rich would accept a string (e.g. :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` and :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`).\ndocs/source/markup.rst:10:    python -m rich.markup\ndocs/source/markup.rst:15:Console markup uses a syntax inspired by `bbcode <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BBCode>`_. If you write the style (see :ref:`styles`) in square brackets, e.g. ``[bold red]``, that style will apply until it is *closed* with a corresponding ``[/bold red]``.\ndocs/source/markup.rst:30:These markup tags may be use in combination with each other and don't need to be strictly nested. The following example demonstrates overlapping of markup tags:: \ndocs/source/markup.rst:37:Rich will raise :class:`~rich.errors.MarkupError` if the markup contains one of the following errors:\ndocs/source/markup.rst:46:Console markup can output hyperlinks with the following syntax: ``[link=URL]text[/link]``. Here's an example::\ndocs/source/markup.rst:56:Occasionally you may want to print something that Rich would interpret as markup. You can *escape* a tag by preceding it with a backslash. Here's an example::\ndocs/source/markup.rst:67:The function :func:`~rich.markup.escape` will handle escaping of text for you.\ndocs/source/markup.rst:69:Escaping is important if you construct console markup dynamically, with ``str.format`` or f strings (for example). Without escaping it may be possible to inject tags where you don't want them. Consider the following function::\ndocs/source/markup.rst:76:    from rich.markup import escape\ndocs/source/markup.rst:83:If you add an *emoji code* to markup it will be replaced with the equivalent unicode character. An emoji code consists of the name of the emoji surrounded be colons (:). Here's an example::\ndocs/source/markup.rst:103:By default, Rich will render console markup when you explicitly pass a string to :meth:`~rich.console.Print.print` or implicitly when you embed a string in another renderable object such as :class:`~rich.table.Table` or :class:`~rich.panel.Panel`.\ndocs/source/markup.rst:105:Console markup is convenient, but you may wish to disable it if the syntax clashes with the string you want to print. You can do this by setting ``markup=False`` on the :meth:`~rich.console.Print.print` method or on the :class:`~rich.console.Console` constructor.\ndocs/source/markup.rst:111:You can convert a string to styled text by calling :meth:`~rich.text.Text.from_markup`, which returns a :class:`~rich.text.Text` instance you can print or add more styles to.\ndocs/source/logging.rst:19:Rich logs won't render :ref:`console_markup` in logging by default as most libraries won't be aware of the need to escape literal square brackets, but you can enable it by setting ``markup=True`` on the handler. Alternatively you can enable it per log message by supplying the ``extra`` argument as follows::\ndocs/source/logging.rst:21:    log.error(\"[bold red blink]Server is shutting down![/]\", extra={\"markup\": True})\ndocs/source/index.rst:16:   markup.rst\ndocs/source/protocol.rst:15:The easiest way to customize console output for your object is to implement a ``__rich__`` method. This method accepts no arguments, and should return an object that Rich knows how to render, such as a :class:`~rich.text.Text` or :class:`~rich.table.Table`. If you return a plain string it will be rendered as :ref:`console_markup`. Here's an example::\ndocs/source/prompt.rst:9:The prompt may be given as a string (which may contain :ref:`console_markup` and emoji code) or as a :class:`~rich.text.Text` instance.\ndocs/source/reference/markup.rst:1:rich.markup\ndocs/source/reference/markup.rst:4:.. automodule:: rich.markup\nrich/text.py:174:        return self.plain\nrich/text.py:177:        return f\"<text {self.plain!r} {self._spans!r} {self.style!r}>\"\nrich/text.py:189:        return self.plain == other.plain and self._spans == other._spans\nrich/text.py:193:            return other in self.plain\nrich/text.py:195:            return other.plain in self.plain\nrich/text.py:202:                self.plain[offset],\nrich/text.py:215:            start, stop, step = slice.indices(len(self.plain))\nrich/text.py:227:        return cell_len(self.plain)\nrich/text.py:230:    def markup(self) -> str:\nrich/text.py:231:        \"\"\"Get console markup to render this Text.\nrich/text.py:234:            str: A string potentially creating markup tags.\nrich/text.py:236:        from .markup import escape\nrich/text.py:240:        plain = self.plain\nrich/text.py:241:        markup_spans = [\nrich/text.py:247:        markup_spans.sort(key=itemgetter(0, 1))\nrich/text.py:250:        for offset, closing, style in markup_spans:\nrich/text.py:256:        markup = \"\".join(output)\nrich/text.py:257:        return markup\nrich/text.py:260:    def from_markup(\nrich/text.py:271:        \"\"\"Create Text instance from markup.\nrich/text.py:274:            text (str): A string containing console markup.\nrich/text.py:283:            Text: A Text instance with markup rendered.\nrich/text.py:285:        from .markup import render\nrich/text.py:344:            text (str): A string containing console markup.\nrich/text.py:412:        if new_text != self.plain:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
